                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the identity landscape`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is day one of Lowlands Logistics Group. Only <strong>34% identify with the new entity</strong>; 81% still identify with their legacy firm; perceived justice is <strong>5.2/10</strong> and ~250 engineers are at flight risk. No strategy is prescribed. Map where identity threat is highest and <em>why</em> — Stadhaven's pride-and-loss of being absorbed, Nimbus's superiority-and-fear of being slowed. Decide which pending choices carry <em>identity</em> weight (the HQ sign, brands, titles, the platform lead) versus pure operational weight, and set your opening posture and first identity signal.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Continuity vs change: the founding message`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The central choice. Commit the integration's core narrative along the <strong>continuity↔change</strong> spectrum. Giessner's social-identity research is blunt: a perceived <em>sense of continuity</em> of one's pre-merger identity strongly predicts post-merger identification, while the <strong>dominance pattern</strong> — one firm's identity simply replacing the other's — predicts resistance and exit. Build a superordinate “we are Lowlands” identity <em>while honouring valued elements of both legacies</em> — and decide the symbolic landmines (HQ sign, brands, office) deliberately, because symbols move identification more than operations.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Justice in action: tooling, titles, the platform lead`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Messaging meets hard decisions. Resolve three justice flashpoints — which <strong>engineering standards</strong> win, how to harmonise titles and the <strong>~8% pay gap</strong>, and <strong>who leads</strong> the merged platform org — each across the three justice types: <em>distributive</em> (who gets what), <em>procedural</em> (was the process fair) and <em>interactional</em> (were people treated with dignity and explanation). Fair process and respectful leadership buffer identity threat: even an unwelcome outcome retains people when the procedure and explanation are just. Fund the retention actions for the ~250 at-risk engineers.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Land the 100 days`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the 100-day review to the combined board and a town hall of employees: identification movement (toward Lowlands <em>without collapsing legacy pride</em>), retention of the at-risk engineers, the perceived-justice trajectory and integration progress. The board probes the trade-off — “You integrated fast; why did we lose 60 of our best engineers?” or “You kept everyone; why is the roadmap nine months late?” State your integration philosophy and <strong>own the trade-off you made</strong>.`,

                    r1_threat_label:        `Where is identity threat highest, and why?`,
                    r1_weight_label:        `Which pending decisions carry the most identity weight (vs pure operational weight)?`,
                    r1_posture_label:       `The integration office's opening posture and first identity signal.`,
                    r1_voice_label:         `How much employee voice do you build into the first 100 days? (%)`,
                    r1_voice_readout:       `Voice & consultation built into the process:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    threat_both:            `Both, asymmetrically — Stadhaven's pride-and-loss of being absorbed; Nimbus's superiority-and-fear of being slowed down`,
                    threat_stadhaven:       `Only Stadhaven — they're the incumbent being bought, so all the threat is theirs`,
                    threat_nimbus:          `Nimbus — the acquirer feels it earned the deal; they have nothing to fear`,
                    threat_neither:         `Neither in particular — it's a friendly merger, threat is overstated`,
                    weight_symbols:         `The symbolic and people ones — the HQ sign, brand names, titles, and who leads the platform; they signal whose identity counts`,
                    weight_ops:             `The operational ones — tooling and budgets are what really matter to people`,
                    weight_none:            `None carry special identity weight — every decision is just a decision`,
                    posture_listen:         `Listen first, honour both — open with a continuity-honouring signal and structured voice before any hard call`,
                    posture_absorb:         `Move fast and absorb — signal from day one that Lowlands is the new way and the legacy is over`,
                    posture_freeze:         `Freeze everything — touch nothing identity-related, keep both firms fully separate to avoid conflict`,

                    r2_narrative_label:     `Commit the founding narrative along the continuity↔change spectrum.`,
                    r2_symbols_label:       `Handle the symbolic landmines — the Rotterdam HQ sign, the two product brands, the office consolidation.`,
                    r2_pace_label:          `Pace of integration this phase — how fast do you push the blend? (%)`,
                    r2_pace_readout:        `Integration pace:`,
                    r2_pct:                 `%`,
                    r2_pace_hint:           `Too fast on a change-heavy narrative spikes identity threat and exits; too slow on continuity stalls synergy. Pace must match your narrative.`,
                    narrative_dual:         `Dual identity / sense of continuity — build a superordinate “we are Lowlands” identity while explicitly honouring valued elements of both legacies`,
                    narrative_rebrand:      `Full change now — new name, new logo, new ways of working by week two; one new Lowlands identity, the legacy is history`,
                    narrative_separate:     `Full continuity — two brands, two org charts, blend very slowly (if ever); change nothing that anyone is attached to`,
                    symbols_deliberate:     `Decide each deliberately — keep, retire or co-brand each symbol on purpose, because symbols move identification more than operations`,
                    symbols_erase:          `Erase fast — strip the old signs and brand names immediately; they're cosmetic`,
                    symbols_ignore:         `Ignore them — they're trivial, don't waste integration time on signage`,

                    r3_standards_label:     `Which engineering standards / toolchain win?`,
                    r3_pay_label:           `Harmonise titles and the ~8% senior pay gap (Nimbus pays higher).`,
                    r3_lead_label:          `Who leads the merged platform organisation — and how is it decided?`,
                    r3_justice_label:       `How do you make and communicate these three decisions?`,
                    r3_retention_label:     `Targeted retention budget for the ~250 flight-risk engineers (EUR M)`,
                    standards_bestofboth:   `A genuine best-of-both merge — adopt the strongest pieces of each, with both teams in the room`,
                    standards_nimbus:       `Nimbus's stack — it's the objectively more modern toolchain, end of discussion`,
                    standards_stadhaven:    `Stadhaven's stack — reward the incumbent's loyalty and tenure`,
                    pay_levelup:            `Level up to fair common bands with a phased, explained transition — costs budget but reads as fair`,
                    pay_leveldown:          `Level Nimbus down to Stadhaven bands — protect the budget, treat it as a spreadsheet exercise`,
                    pay_freeze:             `Freeze it — leave the gap; nobody loses pay, deal with it later`,
                    lead_fairprocess:       `Best person via a transparent, both-firms process — with the rationale explained face-to-face to the team that loses out`,
                    lead_nimbus:            `The Nimbus rising star — most qualified on paper; announce it, move on`,
                    lead_stadhaven:         `The Stadhaven veteran — reward fifteen years of loyalty regardless of fit`,
                    justice_threefold:      `All three justice types — fair process (procedural), voice for those affected, and leaders explaining the why face-to-face (interactional)`,
                    justice_intranet:       `Post the outcomes on the intranet — distributive fairness is what counts, the rest is noise`,
                    justice_silent:         `Announce the decisions with no explanation — let the results speak for themselves`,
                    retention_full:         `€6M — targeted retention, growth paths and stay-conversations across the at-risk population`,
                    retention_partial:      `€3M — modest retention packages for the very top names only`,
                    retention_none:         `€0 — assume the narrative alone keeps them`,

                    r4_board_label:         `Facing the board / town hall — what do you lead with?`,
