                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_both:          `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> Threat is on both sides, asymmetrically: Stadhaven feels pride-and-loss at being absorbed, Nimbus a superiority-and-fear of being slowed. Name both and you can design a signal that speaks to each — miss one and you'll be surprised by where the exits come from.`,
                    coach_r1_stadhaven:     `<strong>You've only seen half the board.</strong> Yes, Stadhaven feels absorbed — but Nimbus's people fear a 28-year incumbent will slow them to a crawl. If your first signal only reassures one side, the other reads it as a verdict against them.`,
                    coach_r1_nimbus:        `<strong>“The acquirer has nothing to fear” is exactly the blind spot.</strong> Nimbus fears being dragged into legacy process; Stadhaven fears erasure. The deepest threat sits with the firm being absorbed — and you've pointed at the wrong group.`,
                    coach_r1_neither:       `<strong>“Friendly merger, threat overstated” is how 250 engineers leave quietly.</strong> 81% still identify with their legacy firm and justice is 5.2/10 — that <em>is</em> identity threat. Underestimate it now and you'll be doing retention by exit interview.`,
                    coach_r1_ops:           `<strong>Tooling and budgets matter — but they're not where the identity weight sits.</strong> The HQ sign, the brand names, the titles and who leads the platform are read as signals of whose identity counts. Treat those as operational and you'll move identification by accident.`,
                    coach_r1_nosymbol:      `<strong>“Every decision is just a decision” underrates the symbols.</strong> To employees, a sign or a brand name is identity made visible — which is why a small symbolic choice can move identification more than a big operational one. Decide which symbols are loaded before you touch them.`,
                    coach_r1_absorb:        `<strong>Opening with “Lowlands is the new way, the legacy is over” is the dominance signal.</strong> On day one, before anyone trusts you, that reads to Stadhaven as erasure — identity threat spikes and your best people start updating their CVs. Where in this is anyone's sense of continuity?`,
                    coach_r1_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing everything avoids conflict and buys nothing.</strong> Touch nothing and you've signalled there's no “one company” to identify with — separation, not integration. Continuity isn't the same as never moving.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read threat on both sides, named the symbolic stakes, and opened by listening and honouring both before any hard call. That's the posture that lets a superordinate identity grow instead of being imposed — and it lowers threat from day one.`,
                    coach_r2_dual:          `<strong>The dual-identity path is the modelled choice.</strong> A superordinate “we are Lowlands” identity built <em>while</em> honouring both legacies gives people a sense of continuity — Giessner's strongest predictor of post-merger identification. Identification climbs and threat falls without asking anyone to abandon who they were.`,
                    coach_r2_dualfast:      `<strong>Right narrative, too much throttle.</strong> The dual story works only if people feel their continuity is real — push the pace to the floor and even an honouring message starts to feel like change being done <em>to</em> them. Ease off and let the superordinate identity set.`,
                    coach_r2_dualslow:      `<strong>Honouring continuity, but barely integrating.</strong> The dual narrative is meant to <em>enable</em> the blend, not stall it. At this pace you protect feelings while the board's EUR 22M sits untouched. Continuity and progress have to move together.`,
                    coach_r2_rebrand:       `<strong>New name, new logo, new ways of working by week two — decisive, and the dominance trap.</strong> Your Stadhaven engineers, who identified with that firm for fifteen years, just read it as erasure. Identification with Lowlands actually <em>dropped</em> and flight risk climbed. Giessner is blunt: dominance maximises identity threat.`,
                    coach_r2_separate:      `<strong>Two brands, two org charts, blend later — nobody's upset, and nothing integrates.</strong> Honouring continuity isn't the same as never integrating. It's heading for day 80 with zero synergy realised and the board's EUR 22M untouched. Where's the superordinate identity that makes this one company?`,
                    coach_r2_symbolerase:   `<strong>Your narrative honours continuity but your symbols don't.</strong> Stripping the old signs and brand names overnight contradicts the message — employees believe the signage, not the slide. Decide each symbol deliberately so it says what you mean.`,
                    coach_r2_symbolignore:  `<strong>You called the signs and brands trivial and moved on.</strong> To employees they're identity made visible — which is exactly why a small symbolic choice can move identification more than a big operational one. Which symbols did you let signal something you didn't intend?`,
                    coach_r3_justice:       `<strong>That's justice as a set.</strong> A genuine best-of-both merge, fair levelled bands explained in a phased transition, the platform lead chosen by transparent process — and all of it made <em>with</em> voice and face-to-face explanation. Even the people who didn't get their way will stay, because the process and the respect were real.`,
                    coach_r3_technical:     `<strong>You picked the “objectively better” toolchain and the most qualified lead — both Nimbus.</strong> On a spreadsheet, fine. To Stadhaven that's three signals in a row that their identity lost. The decisions may be right; the <em>process and explanation</em> are what retain people. Where's your procedural and interactional justice story?`,
                    coach_r3_intranet:      `<strong>You harmonised pay and titles and posted it on the intranet.</strong> Procedurally, did anyone affected have a voice? Interactionally, did a leader explain the why face-to-face? Distributive fairness without procedure and explanation reads as a verdict, not a decision.`,
                    coach_r3_silent:        `<strong>Announcing the decisions with no explanation is the surest way to lose people.</strong> Silence after a hard call reads as “your view didn't matter.” Even a reasonable outcome pushes your best engineers to the exit when the procedure and the explanation are missing.`,
                    coach_r3_payfreeze:     `<strong>Freezing the 8% gap defers the unfairness, it doesn't resolve it.</strong> The senior Stadhaven engineers know Nimbus is paid more for the same level — leaving it untouched reads as the merger ratifying the gap. Fairness deferred is injustice felt.`,
                    coach_r3_noretention:   `<strong>No retention budget on a 250-person flight risk is a bet on the narrative alone.</strong> Identity work moves the odds, but the top 50 have offers in their inbox this quarter. Fund the stay-conversations and growth paths, or you'll lose the talent the synergies depend on.`,
                    coach_r4_continuity:    `<strong>Continuity-into-superordinate is the philosophy that holds.</strong> One new identity built while honouring both legacies means integration was <em>enabled</em>, not imposed. That's the story the board and the town hall can both believe — and the one that kept your engineers.`,
                    coach_r4_dominance:     `<strong>“The strongest identity should win” is the philosophy that lost you the absorbed firm.</strong> Mergers aren't won by the fastest mover; they're won by the one whose people stay. You can defend dominance to the board, but the empty desks in the platform team are the rebuttal.`,
                    coach_r4_separation:    `<strong>“Never force a blend” protected feelings and lost the synergy.</strong> Identity work is supposed to enable integration, not replace it. You can tell the town hall everyone's happy; you'll have to tell the board why the EUR 22M is still on the table.`,
                    coach_r4_speed:         `<strong>Leading with speed invites the obvious probe.</strong> “You integrated fast; why did we lose 60 of our best engineers?” Hitting the roadmap means nothing if the people who deliver it have gone. The board will want the human number, not the Gantt chart.`,
                    coach_r4_harmony:       `<strong>Leading with harmony invites the other probe.</strong> “You kept everyone; why is the roadmap nine months late?” Respected-and-comfortable isn't the deliverable — integration that progresses is. Harmony that defers every hard call is its own failure.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>That owns the trade-off honestly.</strong> Naming what the identity work bought, what it cost, and how you balanced the two is exactly what a board trusts. You didn't chase one KPI or blame the cultures — you ran integration deliberately, and the four numbers show it.`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>Pointing at the one KPI that looks best is how boards stop trusting integration leaders.</strong> They can see all four numbers. Defend the favourable one and downplay the rest, and they'll assume the rest is worse than it is.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the legacy cultures is the integration office abdicating its job.</strong> The resistance you're describing was identity threat — which managing identity was supposed to address. The cultures weren't the problem; how you led them through the threat was the test.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read threat on both sides; named the symbolic stakes; opened by listening.`,
