                    insight_integration:    `Integration achieved: you ran the genuine integration pattern — a superordinate Lowlands identity built on a real sense of continuity, fair process across all three justice types, and a blend that actually progressed. Identification rose without collapsing legacy pride, the at-risk engineers stayed, and the synergies are intact.`,
                    insight_dominance:      `Dominance trap: a fast “one new identity” that Stadhaven read as erasure spiked identity threat and drove exits. Giessner's blunt finding held — dominance maximises threat for the absorbed group, and the talent the deal depended on walked.`,
                    insight_separation:     `Separation paralysis: you kept both identities separate to avoid conflict and realised almost no synergy. Honouring continuity isn't the same as never integrating — the board's EUR 22M stayed on the table while the roadmap stalled.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed integration: some of the identity work landed, but the narrative, justice and pace didn't fully reinforce each other — enough seams for threat to persist and for the board to question whether the synergies are safe.`,
                    insight_toptalent:      `You lost the critical top 50 engineers: that converts directly into a 6–9 month roadmap slip and the EUR 22M of synergies the deal was built on. An exit is irreversible — identification, justice and flight risk were the leading indicators that would have warned you in time.`,
                    insight_justice_low:    `Perceived justice never recovered: the hard decisions were made or communicated without enough procedural and interactional fairness, so even reasonable outcomes read as verdicts and pushed people out.`,
                    insight_ident_low:      `Identification with Lowlands stayed low: people were never given a credible sense of continuity, so the superordinate identity never formed and the legacy loyalties hardened against the new entity.`,
                    insight_harmony_cost:   `You protected the people but stalled the integration: identification and retention are healthy while the roadmap is behind. Identity work is meant to enable integration, not replace it — harmony that defers every hard call still misses the synergy.`,
                    insight_fast_cost:      `You hit the roadmap but lost the people: integration progressed while the at-risk engineers walked. Speed without retention is a hollow win — the synergies live in the talent, not the Gantt chart.`,

                    hint_toptalent:         `You've lost the critical top 50 — the roadmap is now capped and the EUR 22M is at risk. Lower identity threat fast with fair process and a continuity-honouring signal before more go.`,
                    hint_threat:            `Identity threat is dangerously high — exits compound from here. Honour continuity, give people voice, and explain the hard decisions face-to-face before the next round.`,
                    hint_dominance:         `You're running a dominance pattern — identification is falling because the absorbed firm reads it as erasure. Pivot to a sense-of-continuity narrative that honours both legacies.`,
                    hint_separation:        `Integration has stalled — you've honoured continuity but never built the superordinate identity that makes this one company. Push a genuine blend before the synergy window closes.`,
                    hint_justice:           `Perceived justice is too low. Apply all three justice types — fair process, voice, and face-to-face explanation — so even unwelcome outcomes retain people.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — identification, justice and integration are all healthy. Land it: a continuity philosophy and owning the trade-off will hold it through the board.`,

